<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>The Dev Journal | Blog</title>
  <!-- Google Fonts Connection -->
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,600;0,700;1,400&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

  <!-- Blog Header -->
  <header class="blog-header">
    <div class="header-container">
      <h1 class="blog-logo">The Dev Journal</h1>
      <p class="blog-tagline">Thoughts on modern web development, design patterns, and clean code.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- Main Blog Content Feed -->
  <main class="blog-container">
    <div class="blog-feed">
      
      <!-- Article 1 -->
      <article class="post-card">
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span class="post-author">By Alex Rivera</span>
          <span class="meta-divider">•</span>
          <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-07-15">July 15, 2026</time>
        </div>
        <h2 class="post-title">
          <a href="#">Mastering CSS Grid: From Layout Basics to Fluid Layouts</a>
        </h2>
        <p class="post-summary">
          CSS Grid has completely altered the landscape of user interface assembly. In this breakdown, we examine auto-fit behaviors, responsive minmax bounds, and how to eliminate structural breakpoints completely.
        </p>
        <div class="post-footer">
          <a href="#" class="read-more-link">Read Article →</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Article 2 -->
      <article class="post-card">
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span class="post-author">By Jessica Chen</span>
          <span class="meta-divider">•</span>
          <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-07-10">July 10, 2026</time>
        </div>
        <h2 class="post-title">
          <a href="#">The Art of Subtle Micro-interactions</a>
        </h2>
        <p class="post-summary">
          Good design can often be felt rather than explicitly noticed. Learn how leveraging delicate CSS transition parameters and subtle hover scales can significantly improve interface clarity without distracting users.
        </p>
        <div class="post-footer">
          <a href="#" class="read-more-link">Read Article →</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Article 3 -->
      <article class="post-card">
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span class="post-author">By Marcus Vance</span>
          <span class="meta-divider">•</span>
          <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time>
        </div>
        <h2 class="post-title">
          <a href="#">Why Semantic HTML5 Still Matters in 2026</a>
        </h2>
        <p class="post-summary">
          With frameworks dominating development pipelines, structural markup tags sometimes become an afterthought. Let's look at how native semantic structures boost overall reader accessibility and search crawlability index ratings.
        </p>
        <div class="post-footer">
          <a href="#" class="read-more-link">Read Article →</a>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </main>

  <!-- Blog Footer -->
  <footer class="blog-footer">
    <p>&copy; 2026 The Dev Journal. Built with HTML5 & Google Fonts.</p>
  </footer>

</body>
</html>
